<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friedrich Schlegel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1772">1772</a>–<a href="/years/1829">1829</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Friedrich von Schlegel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Friedrich Schlegel was a German literary critic, philosopher and Indologist who was, with his brother August Wilhelm, one of the main figures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a>. The brothers founded the journal Athenaeum, whose fragments and essays stated the principles of the Romantic school, and his novel Lucinde of 1799 caused a scandal. On the Language and Wisdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, published in 1808, compared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Greek, Persian and German, noting many similarities of vocabulary and grammar. Received into the Catholic Church that year, he later served Metternich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> as a diplomat and journalist.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedrich-schlegel.jpg" alt="Friedrich Schlegel" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Gareis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franz_Gareis_Portrait_Friedrich_Schlegel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
